   Operative words being "often" and "expect." Also, even if the book
were inlaid with real metallic silver, you'd have to swing it in such
a way that the silver made contact with the monster's flesh. Even if
your fingers were grippy enough that you could swing a heavy spellbook
like a flyswatter (and /if/ it were really inlaid with metallic silver),
I think it'd be hard to hit with enough force to squish the leather
and make the inlay contact the monster's skin.
</real-world rationale>

   All the comments to the effect of "ha ha, DevTeam missed a case!"
got me incensed enough to actually go look at the source. I count
exactly two expressions, both in weapon.c, that would need to be
changed in order to make spellbooks do silver damage: lines 277
and 300. There's not even any need to post a patch; anyone could
hack the source themselves if they wanted. :-/
   Therefore, I reiterate, it seems clear that the behavior is
intentional. And therefore we /must/ conclude that silver spellbooks
aren't made of silver --- because if they were, they'd do silver
damage, and they don't.

